WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.527
I would like to tell you in advance

00:00:08.527 --> 00:00:12.968
that you are going to hear me say 
the s-word a lot.

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:16.416
And you are going to hear me
say the f-word a lot, too.

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:19.547
So, if some of you are sensitive about
these words,

00:00:19.547 --> 00:00:22.016
you may want to leave right now.

00:00:22.016 --> 00:00:26.499
Okay, I'm going to talk to you 
about success and failure.

00:00:26.499 --> 00:00:32.207
What is failure? What is this f-word
that people talk about all the time?

00:00:32.207 --> 00:00:35.667
I would like you to clap twice
if you think the things I'm about to say

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:39.839
would be a failure in your own lives
or in those around you.

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:42.381
Clap twice if you think being expelled
from school

00:00:42.381 --> 00:00:46.711
and refused by other schools a failure.

00:00:46.711 --> 00:00:50.551
Clap twice if you think studying
for three years a doctoral exam

00:00:50.551 --> 00:00:54.091
just to not pass a failure.

00:00:54.091 --> 00:00:57.831
Clap twice if you think learning
a foreign language for five years

00:00:57.831 --> 00:01:03.131
but still can't speak 
complete sentences a failure.

00:01:03.131 --> 00:01:07.741
Okay, the first person that you might
have clapped for was Albert Einstein.

00:01:07.741 --> 00:01:09.481
Is he a failure?

00:01:09.481 --> 00:01:13.011
He is known for his success,
not for his mistakes.

00:01:13.011 --> 00:01:17.103
But today, I am not going to talk to you
about famous people that we all know.

00:01:17.103 --> 00:01:19.362
I'm going to talk to you 
about Gary Green,

00:01:19.362 --> 00:01:21.228
who most of you may not know,

00:01:21.228 --> 00:01:24.393
and me, who you just met
a few minutes ago.

00:01:24.393 --> 00:01:26.894
So when I was little, Gary Green
went to my school

00:01:26.894 --> 00:01:29.585
and he delivered a speech about failures.

00:01:29.585 --> 00:01:33.965
He is the guy who first introduced me
to look at failures differently.

00:01:33.965 --> 00:01:37.455
He said that he failed 
at his doctoral exam

00:01:37.455 --> 00:01:40.446
after studying three years
of special education.

00:01:40.446 --> 00:01:41.945
And even worse than that,

00:01:41.945 --> 00:01:46.606
his wife and he had planned a party
to celebrate his passing of the exam,

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:48.917
but he was informed that he didn't pass

00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:52.485
and needed to complete an additional year
of graduate school.

00:01:52.485 --> 00:01:55.626
As you are thinking about
"what is he going to do about the party?"

00:01:55.626 --> 00:01:58.725
well, I emailed him 
and asked him just that.

00:01:58.725 --> 00:02:02.637
He answered by saying it would be
cathartic to have a failure party

00:02:02.637 --> 00:02:06.796
where everyone celebrated various
failures in their lives

00:02:06.796 --> 00:02:08.607
and they just had a great time.

00:02:08.607 --> 00:02:11.772
After hearing about this story,
my mindset completely changed.

00:02:11.772 --> 00:02:15.805
I thought to myself not passing
exams were not the end of the world.

00:02:15.805 --> 00:02:20.516
So now, I would like you to all think back
to our clap twice exercise.

00:02:20.516 --> 00:02:21.986
Remember the last one?

00:02:21.986 --> 00:02:25.996
The person who failed at learning
a foreign language for five years was me.

00:02:25.996 --> 00:02:29.396
So would you all look at me right now
and call me a failure?

00:02:29.396 --> 00:02:34.847
When I first went to Argentina,
a Spanish-speaking country,

00:02:34.847 --> 00:02:38.538
I could not speak Spanish,
nor English for that matter.

00:02:38.538 --> 00:02:41.599
I don't know how many of you
had the same experience as I did

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:44.167
when I first started to learn
a new language.

00:02:44.167 --> 00:02:47.038
I needed to do a little Google translate
in my mind

00:02:47.038 --> 00:02:49.916
before I could fluently say it out loud.

00:02:49.916 --> 00:02:51.117
So as you can imagine,

00:02:51.117 --> 00:02:54.838
my first few friends here were very
patient waiting for what I wanted to say.

00:02:54.838 --> 00:02:57.467
My friend will ask me 
what did I eat yesterday?

00:02:57.467 --> 00:03:00.791
Asking about food is the hardest
question ever,

00:03:00.791 --> 00:03:02.439
even harder and mind-provoking

00:03:02.439 --> 00:03:05.881
than the question,
"what is the meaning of life?"

00:03:05.881 --> 00:03:13.491
How am I supposed to know
this little green mini-sized tree

00:03:13.491 --> 00:03:15.842
is called broccoli?

00:03:15.842 --> 00:03:19.492
I would have to translate beans, 
fried chicken and steamed broccoli

00:03:19.492 --> 00:03:20.702
to my friends.

00:03:20.702 --> 00:03:25.112
It's actually a wonder that we're
still friends after all that food talk.

00:03:25.112 --> 00:03:29.445
Anyway, beyond that, I see native
speakers strolling through the halls

00:03:29.445 --> 00:03:33.475
saying complete sentences
and reading a text out loud

00:03:33.475 --> 00:03:37.113
as if they have practiced in front
of the mirror the whole afternoon,

00:03:37.113 --> 00:03:39.896
while I first have to search up
all the exotic words

00:03:39.896 --> 00:03:41.687
that I had never seen in my life,

00:03:41.687 --> 00:03:43.566
click to listen to the pronunciation,

00:03:43.566 --> 00:03:46.397
and repeat the word several times
to remember.

00:03:46.397 --> 00:03:48.496
I remember for an oral assignment 
for school

00:03:48.496 --> 00:03:50.470
how hard I practiced at home,

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.107
but then when I came to school,

00:03:52.107 --> 00:03:55.726
the things I was about to say
just couldn't come out of my mouth.

00:03:55.726 --> 00:03:58.266
I also suffered because of my accent.

00:03:58.266 --> 00:04:01.168
I remember how people couldn't
understand what I wanted to say

00:04:01.168 --> 00:04:02.908
and tell me to spell the words.

00:04:02.908 --> 00:04:09.827
So there I was spelling,
"h-o-w-a-r-e-y-o-u, how are you?"

00:04:09.827 --> 00:04:12.497
Well that kind of conversation
would take forever,

00:04:12.497 --> 00:04:16.286
so I asked my friends to correct me
whenever I sounded weird.

00:04:16.286 --> 00:04:20.957
Gradually, people just stopped asking 
me simple words like chicken or rice.

00:04:20.957 --> 00:04:23.436
At first, I thought I was a failure.

00:04:23.436 --> 00:04:26.547
Maybe I was just not able 
to speak in public.

00:04:26.547 --> 00:04:30.888
But now when I look my language learning,
I look at all the experiences I had,

00:04:30.888 --> 00:04:33.558
I realized I wasn't a failure at all.

00:04:33.558 --> 00:04:36.439
In fact, I was quite successful
in not giving up

00:04:36.439 --> 00:04:39.446
and going to bed crying every night.

00:04:39.446 --> 00:04:43.138
Here's a bit of an odd transition,
so just go with me here.

00:04:43.138 --> 00:04:46.509
Never did I think that I would be
talking about my language learning

00:04:46.509 --> 00:04:49.718
to talk about the founder of Spanx.

00:04:49.718 --> 00:04:51.599
But, here it is.

00:04:51.599 --> 00:04:54.097
Sarah Blakely was talking 
to Forbes Magazine

00:04:54.097 --> 00:04:57.138
and she defined failure as "not trying,"

00:04:57.138 --> 00:05:00.028
but I tried extremely hard 
to learn English.

00:05:00.028 --> 00:05:02.809
So, in her eyes, I wasn't a failure.

00:05:02.809 --> 00:05:06.639
Thank you, founder of Spanx.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.819
Astro Teller, the head of X,

00:05:08.819 --> 00:05:11.989
did a TED talk about his so-called
"moonshot factory"

00:05:11.989 --> 00:05:16.441
where his team and he needed
to come up with solutions

00:05:16.441 --> 00:05:18.808
to solve the world's biggest problems.

00:05:18.808 --> 00:05:20.210
Did it work at first?

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:22.919
No. He said he failed so many times.

00:05:22.919 --> 00:05:26.541
But then what did he do?
He celebrated his failures.

00:05:26.541 --> 00:05:28.070
And then what happened?

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:29.774
He succeeded.

00:05:29.774 --> 00:05:34.043
We have heard about so many famous 
people, successful people, talk about it,

00:05:34.043 --> 00:05:35.670
but what about kids?

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:38.151
The kids that you see on the screen
were in a class

00:05:38.151 --> 00:05:40.624
where everyone gets 
the same amount of stickers

00:05:40.624 --> 00:05:44.016
whether they did well
or not so well on their exams.

00:05:44.016 --> 00:05:47.407
Everyone gets a high-five
when they learned from their mistakes.

00:05:47.407 --> 00:05:50.574
And things are phrased 
very differently in that class.

00:05:50.574 --> 00:05:52.491
They say they had an "oops moment,"

00:05:52.491 --> 00:05:55.161
which I personally think 
is a really good phrase.

00:05:55.161 --> 00:05:57.391
So, you can't say that I didn't warn you

00:05:57.391 --> 00:06:00.211
that I would be using 
the f-word all this time.

00:06:00.211 --> 00:06:03.735
I think TED-Ed Weekend is a really
classy and professional event,

00:06:03.735 --> 00:06:06.783
so I would like to honor the cute
fifth graders

00:06:06.783 --> 00:06:09.160
and use the oops moment from now on.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:14.671
Hey, I haven't failed, you haven't failed,
we just had a little oops moment.

00:06:14.671 --> 00:06:20.823
Just as Sarah Blakley, Gary Green,
Astro Teller, Albert Einstein and me,

00:06:20.823 --> 00:06:24.513
every one of us has some oops moments
in our lives.

00:06:24.513 --> 00:06:27.645
But however, instead of considering
them as failures,

00:06:27.645 --> 00:06:29.743
what if we celebrate them?

00:06:29.743 --> 00:06:33.563
Imagine a world if everyone
celebrated our oops moments,

00:06:33.563 --> 00:06:37.142
imagine if we all have 
an oops moment party.

00:06:37.142 --> 00:06:38.723
How will we all change

00:06:38.723 --> 00:06:42.313
and how will the world be different 
at the end of the day?

00:06:42.313 --> 00:06:45.182
I would like to see with a little bit
of an experiment.

00:06:45.182 --> 00:06:47.574
I would like to ask the people
in the front row,

00:06:47.574 --> 00:06:53.263
Hi, can you share an oops moment with me?

00:06:53.263 --> 00:06:55.984
Flunking a test.

00:06:55.984 --> 00:06:58.247
Oh, okay, did you learn from it?

00:06:58.247 --> 00:06:59.455
Definitely.

00:06:59.455 --> 00:07:02.923
Okay, high-five, that's great.

00:07:02.923 --> 00:07:06.044
Hey, that high-five actually 
feels really good.

00:07:06.044 --> 00:07:09.253
So now I would like you 
to turn to the people around you

00:07:09.253 --> 00:07:12.032
and share one oops moment
and high-five them.

00:07:12.032 --> 00:07:21.035
But just one because 
I have a speech to finish.

00:07:21.035 --> 00:07:25.914
Okay, so as I stand here
in front of all of you today,

00:07:25.914 --> 00:07:31.504
sweating, even shivering a little,
I still consider myself successful,

00:07:31.504 --> 00:07:34.105
even though I couldn't 
fall asleep yesterday

00:07:34.105 --> 00:07:36.786
and I might have messed up 
a couple of lines,

00:07:36.786 --> 00:07:38.325
but why should I care?

00:07:38.325 --> 00:07:40.535
I'm doing speeches 
that I was once scared of,

00:07:40.535 --> 00:07:44.326
I'm talking to you all using the language
I wasn't confident in.

00:07:44.326 --> 00:07:47.175
Failure? Yeah right!

